Since you are using Termux, ensure you have run termux-setup-storage first so that the environment has permission to access your files.

Here is the step-by-step workflow to catalog your files and pull them to your PC.

Step 1: Generate the Path List (Run in Termux)
Run this command to search both your Internal storage (/sdcard) and your External SD Card (usually located in /storage/). It will output all .mp3 and .m4a file paths into a file named songpaths.txt located in your internal storage root.

Bash
# Find files and save to songpaths.txt
find /sdcard /storage -type f \( -name "*.mp3" -o -name "*.m4a" \) > /sdcard/songpaths.txt
-type f: Looks for files only (ignores folders).

-name "*.mp3" -o -name "*.m4a": The -o flag means "OR," so it grabs both file types.

Step 2: Get the list to your PC
Now, use ADB from your desktop to pull that list over so you can process it:

Bash
adb pull /sdcard/songpaths.txt ./
Step 3: The Pull Loop (Run on your Desktop)
Because you want to automate the pulling process based on the text file, you need a script on your desktop. Choose the one that matches your OS:

If you are on Windows (PowerShell)
Open PowerShell in the folder where you have songpaths.txt and run:

PowerShell
# Create a destination directory
mkdir .\AllSongs

# Loop through the text file and pull each path
Get-Content songpaths.txt | ForEach-Object {
    if ($_ -ne "") {
        adb pull "$_" .\AllSongs
    }
}
If you are on Linux or macOS (Bash)
Run this command in your terminal:

Bash
mkdir -p ./AllSongs
while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -z "$line" ] && continue
    adb pull "$line" ./AllSongs
done < songpaths.txt
Important Considerations for your workflow:
Duplicate Filenames: adb pull generally preserves the directory structure. If you have two different files named Song.mp3 in different folders on your phone, the second one might overwrite the first one on your PC, or adb will create nested folders (e.g., .\AllSongs\Downloads\Song.mp3 and .\AllSongs\Music\Song.mp3).

The "Flatten" Fix: Once the pull is complete, you can use a command on your PC to move everything into one folder and rename them if necessary. On Linux/macOS, use: find ./AllSongs -type f -exec mv -t ./AllSongs/ {} + (This will move all files from sub-folders into the root of AllSongs).

Permissions: If find in Termux returns "Permission Denied" errors, it is likely trying to access system folders. You can safely ignore these errors, or modify the command to exclude the /storage/emulated system directories if needed, but the command provided above should be sufficient for standard user media.

The "Intelligence" Factor: As we discussed earlier, once these are all in one folder on your PC, running MusicBrainz Picard on that single folder is infinitely faster than doing it folder-by-folder. It will handle the renaming of your file672.mp3 files in one go while they are all sitting in that AllSongs directory.

